# Auto DXVK

A graphical application (WPF) that automates the installation of [DXVK](https://github.com/doitsujin/dxvk) into Windows games. It downloads the selected release, detects the game architecture and DirectX version, and copies the appropriate DLL files to the game folder.

Repository: https://github.com/Khotyz/AutoDXVK

## Requirements

- Windows 10 (1809 through 22H2) or Windows 11 (all versions)
- Windows PowerShell 5.1 (included in the operating system). PowerShell 7 is not required.
- .NET Framework 4.7.2 or later (already included in the versions above)
- `tar.exe` from Windows (available since Windows 10 1803)
- Internet access for querying the GitHub API and downloading DXVK
- A GPU and driver with Vulkan support

## Structure

    AutoDXVK/
    ├── AutoDXVK.ps1
    ├── lang/
    │   ├── en.json
    │   ├── pt-br.json
    │   └── es.json
    └── README.md

The `lang` folder must reside next to `AutoDXVK.ps1`.

## Saving AutoDXVK.ps1 with the correct encoding

The script uses only ASCII characters, but saving it as UTF-8 with BOM prevents any reading issues in Windows 10 consoles with legacy code pages.

1. Open `AutoDXVK.ps1` in Notepad, VS Code, or Notepad++.
2. Notepad: File > Save As > Encoding: UTF-8 with BOM.
3. VS Code: click `UTF-8` in the status bar, then Save with encoding, then UTF-8 with BOM.
4. Notepad++: Encoding menu, then UTF-8-BOM.

The language `.json` files must remain in UTF-8 (with or without BOM), as they contain accented characters.

## Running from a local copy

Open PowerShell in the project folder and execute:

    Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy Bypass
    .\AutoDXVK.ps1

If the files were obtained from a download, unblock them beforehand:

    Get-ChildItem -Recurse | Unblock-File

It is also possible to start the script without changing the current session policy:

    powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -STA -File .\AutoDXVK.ps1

Games installed in protected folders, such as `C:\Program Files`, require PowerShell to be started as administrator.

## Running online without downloading manually

The command below downloads the script and the language files to a temporary folder and starts the application. Paste the entire line into any PowerShell window:

    $b='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Khotyz/AutoDXVK/main';$d=Join-Path $env:TEMP 'AutoDXVK';New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path (Join-Path $d 'lang') -Force|Out-Null;Invoke-WebRequest "$b/AutoDXVK.ps1" -OutFile (Join-Path $d 'AutoDXVK.ps1');'en','pt-br','es'|%{Invoke-WebRequest "$b/lang/$_.json" -OutFile (Join-Path $d "lang\$_.json")};powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -STA -File (Join-Path $d 'AutoDXVK.ps1')

The files are stored under `%TEMP%\AutoDXVK`. Each execution overwrites the previous copy. The `lang` folder is placed alongside the script, so no manual setup is required.

## Packaging with PS2EXE

    Install-Module ps2exe -Scope CurrentUser
    Invoke-PS2EXE .\AutoDXVK.ps1 .\AutoDXVK.exe -noConsole -STA

Keep the `lang` folder next to the `.exe`.

## Usage

1. Select the DXVK version. The list contains the five most recent releases from GitHub. The Refresh button reloads the list.
2. Click Browse and select the game `.exe`. The application displays the architecture, the detected DirectX version, and the DLL files that will be installed.
3. Click Install DXVK.

## What the script does

- Downloads the `.tar.gz` to `Downloads\Auto DXVK\` and extracts it into the same folder. The folder is not removed after installation.
- If DLL files already exist in the game folder, it asks for confirmation before overwriting and saves the originals in `DXVK_Backup`. An existing backup is never overwritten.
- Creates an empty `dxvk.conf` in the game folder if one does not already exist.
- Displays a toast notification at the end, with `MessageBox` as a fallback.

## DLL files per API

| Detected API | Copied DLL files |
| --- | --- |
| D3D8 | d3d8.dll |
| D3D9 | d3d9.dll |
| D3D10 / D3D10.1 | d3d10.dll, d3d10_1.dll, d3d10core.dll, dxgi.dll |
| D3D11 | d3d11.dll, dxgi.dll |
| D3D12 | d3d12.dll, dxgi.dll |

Only the DLL files that exist in the downloaded package are copied.

## API detection

The script inspects the selected executable and, when the result is inconclusive, additional binaries located in the same folder or in adjacent directories. The following scenarios are covered:

- Launchers whose executable is a small stub. The script inspects sibling `.exe` files and files inside `Binaries\Win64` or `Binaries\Win32`.
- Unity games. `UnityPlayer.dll` is inspected when the main `.exe` does not reveal the API.
- Unreal Engine games. Executables whose names end in `-Win64-Shipping.exe`, `-Win32-Shipping.exe`, or `-Shipping.exe` are inspected.

When multiple DirectX APIs are present, D3D11 takes precedence over D3D12. This matches the case of Unity and Unreal Engine games, which often expose both APIs but render through D3D11 on most configurations.

## Appearance by Windows version

- Windows 11: Mica effect and rounded corners through DWM. On builds earlier than 22621 the script attempts the alternative Mica attribute.
- Windows 10: solid light or dark background according to the system theme, without Mica.
- Accent color is read from the system, with a fallback to `#0078D4`.

## Known limitations

- **Executables packaged or protected:** DirectX detection reads DLL names inside the file. Executables protected by packers, DRM, or dynamic loading may not reveal the API. In this case the application warns the user and does not allow installation.
- **Text based detection:** the presence of `dxgi.dll` is treated as D3D11, and the highest precedence API wins. Games that reference multiple APIs may be classified differently from the one actually used at runtime.
- **Direct3D 12:** DXVK does not implement D3D12. That is the role of vkd3d-proton. DXVK packages do not include `d3d12.dll`, so installation for D3D12 games is interrupted with an explanatory message.
- **Direct3D 8:** `d3d8.dll` is only present in recent DXVK releases. On older versions, select a different release.
- **Direct3D 10:** in DXVK 2.x, `d3d10.dll` and `d3d10_1.dll` do not exist, and `d3d10core.dll` depends on `d3d11.dll`. In this case the script also copies `d3d11.dll` from the package.
- **Anti-cheat:** games with anti-cheat may block or ban DLL modifications. Use at your own risk.
- **GitHub rate limit:** the public API limits unauthenticated requests. In case of an error, wait and use Refresh.
- **Reverting changes:** to uninstall, restore the files from `DXVK_Backup` or delete the copied DLL files.

## Summary of recent changes

- Added fallback inspection of rendering binaries when the selected executable does not reveal a DirectX API. This resolves the frequent case of Unity and Unreal Engine games whose main `.exe` is only a stub or a launcher.
- DirectX resolution now applies a precedence order that favors D3D11 over D3D12 when both APIs are present. This reflects the actual rendering path of most Unity and Unreal Engine titles.
- Architecture and library detection now tolerate files that are locked by other processes and continue scanning the remaining candidates instead of aborting the analysis.
- Installation now uses a lookup table for architecture folder names, aligning the structure with the rest of the configuration data in the script.